Home Network Infrastructure Deployment & Security Hardening

This file contains my overall process of installing, configuring, and testing/attacking my household Google Nest Router and mesh topology. It will contain
lessons learned, new discoveries, issues to troubleshoot, topics based, and supportive images to provide context and integrity to my project.

1. Network Deployment
  - Router Installation/Configuration
        The beginning of this project started with intent to upgrade overall LAN/WAN infrastructure by upgrading internet
        speeds from 500 mbps to 1 gbps, as well as establishing a mesh topological network for higher wireless connectivity and less
        deadzones. Thus, the Google Nest Router was purchased along with two other Google Nest Routers to act as wireless
        access points (WAPs). Once routers were delivered, the old router was disconnected, and the new main Google Nest 
        Router was connected to the Quantum Fiber ISP Modem via cat-6 ethernet cable, physically establishing a connection
        capable of over 1 gbps. The Google Home software application was subsequently downloaded and configured with home-owner
        credentials with administrative access to network controls. The new Wi-Fi began running with a new SSID and
        WPA2-PSK security.
  - Mesh Topology Deployment
        Upon successful Wi-Fi development, the two WAPs were plugged in at further points within the household to power up and connect to the main
        router in the office.
